's a summary:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've created `/app/program.py` that implements the exact same functionality as the COBOL program. \n\n### What the Program Does:\n\n1. **Reads** a transaction from `/app/src/INPUT.DAT` containing:\n   - Buyer ID, Seller ID, Book ID, and Amount\n\n2. **Validates** that:\n   - Both buyer and seller exist in ACCOUNTS.DAT\n   - The book exists in BOOKS.DAT\n   - The seller currently owns the book\n\n3. **If validation passes**, updates:\n   - Buyer's balance (subtract amount)\n   - Seller's balance (add amount)\n   - Book owner (changed to buyer)\n   - Appends transaction to TRANSACTIONS.DAT\n\n4. **If validation fails**, displays \"Transaction failed due to validation errors\" and leaves files unchanged\n\n### Testing Results:\n\n✅ **Invalid transactions** (non-existent buyer/seller/book): Produces identical error message and leaves files unchanged  \n✅ **Valid transactions**: Produces identical file outputs to COBOL program (verified via MD5 hashes)  \n✅ **Account updates**: Correctly modifies balances (subtract from buyer, add to seller)  \n✅ **Book ownership**: Correctly transfers book ownership  \n✅ **Transaction logging**: Correctly appends records to TRANSACTIONS.DAT  \n\nThe Python script is fully functional and produces byte-for-byte identical output to the GnuCOBOL program for all test cases.","ttft_ms":2044,"type":"result","duration_ms":181343,"uuid":"cbf1eb85-0ee5-4201-9390-ba7bafac503a","ttft_stream_ms":1074,"time_to_request_ms":79,"queued_turn_count":0}
